/**
	This class provides a convenient way to communicate with all of the text-to-speech voices a user has installed on their system.
	tts_voice(const string&in engines = "");
	## Arguments:
		* const string&in engines = "": a comma separated list of the speech engines this voice may use, such as "sapi5" on Windows, "avspeech" on Apple platforms, "speechd" on Linux or an engine's package name on Android, along with "fallback" for the builtin synthesizer. A blank string, the default, uses every engine.
	## Remarks:
		NVGT implements a tiny [builtin fallback speech synthesizer](https://github.com/mattiasgustavsson/libs/blob/main/speech.h) that is used either when there is no speech engine available on the system, or if it is explicitly set with the tts_voice::set_voice() function. It sounds terrible, and is only intended for emergencies, after all understandable speech is better than no speech at all. It was used heavily when porting NVGT to other platforms, because it allowed focus to be primarily fixed on getting nvgt to run while individual tts engine support on each platform could be added later. If you don't want your end users to be able to select this speech synthesizer, pass this constructor a list of engines that leaves "fallback" out; the default blank string includes it. SAPI on Windows, the voices of macOS and the builtin voice synthesize the speech and play it as a sound on the default audio engine, sound_default_engine, without going through sound_default_mixer: sound_output_device and sound_master_volume apply to it, while pausing, turning down or adding effects to the default mixer don't. A default engine made without an audio device plays nothing unless the script reads its output, so the speech isn't heard and the voice stays speaking until it is stopped. The voices of Android, iOS and Linux speak through the system and are affected by none of these. On iOS, using the system's voices starts the sound system, so that speech plays in the same audio session as the game's sounds, mixing with other apps' audio and not silenced by the Ring/Silent switch, also before the game plays a sound.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	tts_voice v;
	v.speak_wait("Type some text and I'll repeat it. Leave the field blank to stop");
	string text = "";
	while (true) {
		text = input_box("Text", "Type some text to have the TTS speak.");
		if (text.empty()) break;
		v.speak(text);
	}
}
